      "content": "<h3>What is a LinkedHashSet?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>LinkedHashSet</strong> is a <code>HashSet</code> that <strong>remembers the order in which you added the elements</strong>. You still get no duplicates and fast lookups, but when you print or loop, the elements come out exactly in <strong>insertion order</strong>. Think of a visitor register: each fort is written once, in the order of the first visit.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Package <code>java.util</code>. It <strong>extends HashSet</strong> and, since Java 21, also implements <code>SequencedSet</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>No duplicates</strong> (the same rule as HashSet). Adding an element that is already present does <strong>not</strong> change its position.</li>\n<li><strong>Predictable order:</strong> iteration follows insertion order.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast:</strong> add, remove and contains are O(1) on average, with a small extra cost to maintain the order.</li>\n<li><strong>One null</strong> is allowed. <strong>Not thread-safe.</strong></li>\n<li>Use it when you need <strong>uniqueness and a stable order</strong>: removing duplicates from a list while keeping the order, caches, \"recently seen\" lists, report output.</li>\n</ul>"
