      "content": "<h3>Benefits and advantages</h3>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> a LinkedHashSet is a HashSet that <em>remembers the order you added things in</em>. You get \"no duplicates\" and a predictable order together.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Predictable order:</strong> loops and <code>toString()</code> always show elements in insertion order.</li>\n<li><strong>Still fast:</strong> <code>add</code>, <code>remove</code> and <code>contains</code> are O(1) on average, like a HashSet.</li>\n<li><strong>Best way to remove duplicates but keep order:</strong> <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(new LinkedHashSet&lt;&gt;(list))</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Java 21 extras:</strong> <code>getFirst</code>, <code>getLast</code>, <code>removeFirst</code>, <code>addFirst</code> and <code>reversed()</code> make it easy to treat it like a queue or a history list.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeatable output:</strong> tests and printed results look the same on every run.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> internally a HashMap whose entries are also chained in a doubly linked list. Iteration is O(size), unlike HashSet, whose iteration is O(capacity + size). Re-adding an existing element does <strong>not</strong> change its position.</p>"
