      "content": "<h3>Disadvantages and when NOT to use it</h3>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> the order has a price, and it is not sorted order.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>More memory than HashSet:</strong> every entry carries two extra links (before and after).</li>\n<li><strong>Slightly slower adds and removes</strong> because the links must be updated.</li>\n<li><strong>Insertion order is not sorted order:</strong> if you want A to Z, use a TreeSet.</li>\n<li><strong>No index:</strong> still no <code>get(i)</code> or <code>indexOf</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Same rules as HashSet:</strong> good <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code>, no changing elements after adding, not thread-safe.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> re-inserting to \"refresh\" an element needs <code>remove</code> then <code>add</code> (two operations). For a real LRU cache a <code>LinkedHashMap</code> with access order is a better fit.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;\">\n<tr><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Use a LinkedHashSet when</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Use something else when</th></tr>\n<tr><td>You need uniqueness AND insertion order</td><td>Order does not matter: <strong>HashSet</strong> (less memory)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You show history, steps or a route once each</td><td>You need sorted order or range search: <strong>TreeSet</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>You dedupe a list and keep the first-seen order</td><td>You need duplicates or positions: <strong>ArrayList</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>You want repeatable output for tests</td><td>You need an LRU cache: <strong>LinkedHashMap</strong> (access order)</td></tr>\n</table>"
